The National Biodiversity Future Center (NBFC) focuses on the Mediterranean region, considered a biodiversity hotspot, in order to address global challenges related to the following:
-Knowledge and protection of Italian biodiversity;
-Monitoring of different species, including rare, endangered and exotic pest species;
-Development of appropriate 'Restoration Ecology' strategies also with the aim of achieving the goals of the Nature Restoration Law;
-Enhancement of biodiversity through innovation actions that produce economic, environmental and social value.
Through targeted research and adoption of new technologies, NBFC provides intervention bodies and territorial administrations with the scientific basis for the establishment and management of protected areas, the design and implementation of efficient monitoring systems, the implementation of ecological restoration programs, and the enhancement of biodiversity to obtain new food, medical, cosmetics, and new materials. An entire sector of the center is dedicated to innovation, the involvement of national and international companies and institutions to support sustainable production processes, stimulate the creation of start-ups and expand green jobs. In this area, the center works with industrial supply chains by identifying process innovations and studying innovative biotechnological solutions that make it possible to promote circular productions, enhancing the value of raw materials and revitalizing strategic sectors for Italy such as the nursery sector, fishing, aquaculture and the production of bioactive compounds. The NBFC is therefore proposed as a national and international reference for generating knowledge and value from biodiversity and transferring them to the territory with the aim of implementing ecosystem services, promoting the well-being of people and generating social and environmental economic value for society.

eXact lab Srl is a privately held technology company spun off in 2011 from SISSA (Scuola Internazionale Superiore di Studi Avanzati, Trieste). It is registered as a Benefit Company, combining commercial objectives with measurable environmental and social value creation. The board comprises Prof. Stefano Cozzini (co-founder), Francesco De Giorgi (co-founder, CEO), and Giuseppe Piero Brandino, PhD (CTO).
eXact lab specialises in scientific computing, high-performance computing (HPC), scientific software development, and data infrastructure. Its staff come almost entirely from scientific and research backgrounds &mdash; nearly half hold a PhD in Physics, Mathematics, Geophysics, or Civil Engineering &mdash; giving the company the technical depth to operate at the intersection of research and engineering. Its core services are:
software design and optimisation for large-scale data processing;

HPC infrastructure design, deployment, and management;

software performance optimisation;

AI and machine learning for scientific and industrial applications;

training in technologies related to the activities above.

The company is organised in two main business units. The Operations BU covers provisioning, administration, and management of HPC infrastructure. The Development BU covers all software development activities and is divided into Data Science and HPC sub-units. As is typical of a SME, boundaries between units are intentionally permeable: project teams are cross-disciplinary and staff contribute across the full stack, so that expertise in HPC, data engineering, and scientific software is distributed rather than siloed.

eXact lab has extensive experience across the full value chain of Earth Observation data: from devising and deploying the compute-intensive processing pipelines that turn raw sensor data into scientific products, to designing and managing the data infrastructure that makes those products discoverable and accessible, to building the programmatic interfaces that end users rely on in operational workflows. This end-to-end familiarity &mdash; spanning production, distribution, and use of EO data &mdash; is directly relevant to a contract requiring user-driven maintenance and development of a key component of the Copernicus Marine Service ecosystem such as the toolbox.

Vilnius University Methodical STEAM Education Centre (Vilniaus universiteto Metodinis STEAM ugdymo centras) is Lithuania&rsquo;s largest and most advanced hub for multidisciplinary STEAM education. As part of the national STEAM Center Network, it serves as a dynamic bridge between research, education, and industry. As a department of one of Central and Eastern Europe&rsquo;s oldest and most prestigious universities, the Centre builds on Vilnius University's robust research infrastructure. Eight cutting-edge laboratories &mdash; Robotics, Visual Programming, Engineering, Fabrication, Light (Laser) Technologies, Physics and Astronomy, Life Sciences, and Chemistry &mdash; empower students aged 13&ndash;19 to engage in hands-on scientific exploration, creative problem-solving, and innovation. 
Our core activities include: 
Hands-on formal and non-formal STEAM education for schoolchildren; 
Professional STEAM training and upskilling for teachers; 
Development of innovative educational resources and methodologies; 
Research-driven evaluation of STEAM learning effectiveness.

Through strong collaboration with over 100 educational institutions, businesses, and the public sector, the Centre actively promotes inclusive, future-oriented STEAM education &mdash; fostering digital literacy, computational thinking, gender balance, and creativity. By aligning with national and European priorities, the Vilnius University Methodical STEAM Education Centre plays a vital role in advancing high-quality, inclusive education and shaping the next generation of scientists, engineers, and innovators.
